WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
My name is Alexander Pym

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.980
I'm an investigator here at K-RITH, KwaZulu-Natal Research Institute for

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.540
TB and HIV

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.170
So tell me about the project you're
working on to come up with better ways

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.170
to detect drug-resistant tuberculosis.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.340
This morning we were at the hospital
where there's tons of MDR and XDR.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.220
What's the problem that you're trying to
solve? So we want to get people on the

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.449
best treatment as quickly as possible. 
And the problem is--the tests we have

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:29.099
available at the moment

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:34.410
take two to three months and are not very
well developed. And so for two or

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.460
three months

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.450
you have to treat patients with drugs
you don't know where they going to work

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.020
or not

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.930
you may be treating them with five drugs
at work when you may be treating them

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.430
with five that don't.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:49.140
And as a result of this patients can die or
 develop further

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:55.289
resistance. So we're trying to we're
trying to develop a rapid comprehensive

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:58.280
resistance test so a patient can walk
into the hospital

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.600
we can do the test and then within a
week or a few days we can give them the

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.430
best possible treatment

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.320
What's your strategy? How are you going
about developing something that doesn't

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.770
exist and what you have a lot of
equipment here that doesn't exist in

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:19.110
many places in Africa. (yeah) What're you  gonna do? We're working on the genetic code of

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.460
drug resistance.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.270
So instead of growing the the bacteria
in the presence of drugs, which takes a

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.110
long time

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:30.420
we're trying to figure out the bar code
of resistance. Drug resistance arises

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.220
because of a mutation and then you use
sequencing equipment and you come up

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.040
with a barcode of

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.170
resistance. And if we can figure out where
the mutations are and who has them we'll

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.820
be able to tell immediately

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.380
who is resistant or not.  One of the
limitations of working on TB is you

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.260
have to work in a highly controlled
environment

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.610
Because TB is spread in the air. If I'm carrying culture with one of these

00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:01.090
potentially deadly strains and I drop it
on the ground then I can infect you

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:06.140
One of the great things about K-RITH is
that we have some of the best BSL threes

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.200
in South Africa, if not in Africa

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:16.640
We're really focusing in an area where
we have XDR and the epidemics unfolding

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.700
in front of our eyes. So we're in a
great position to take something we

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.590
found in the lab and test it immediately
in the clinic. We can find a new mutation

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.850
we can figure out these strains are
resistant to this drug and then I can go

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.300
to King Dinuzulu Hospital on
Wednesday and say hey look guys

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:37.459
you shouldn't be --- you can't use capriomiacin in these patients because we

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:41.030
found out actually they're susceptible
or vice versa. You shouldn't be using

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.320
this drug because actually we it's
resistant -- we know these bugs are

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:50.510
resistant. And so we can get that
feedback between the lab and clinic

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.590
which is really what I think makes K-RITH  unique

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.900
We have some new drugs and we're

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.370
we're recycling some old drugs and I
think in the next two to three years we

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:06.230
will be able to dramatically shorten
treatment for drug resistance from 18

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.340
months to two years (what it is now) down
to we hope nine months or six months

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.840
that will really make a big impact on on
treating treating drug resistance

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.260
You need this new testing to go hand-in-hand with the roll out. Exactly, exactly

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.439
And particularly as we introduce new
drugs

00:03:21.439 --> 00:03:25.040
we need to be able to rapidly monitor if
someone's become resistant to them and

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.640
that's where i think this these new
genetic tools really be of benefit as well.

